# Semgrep path exclusions for the bedrock module scan. A path listed here is left out of
# the scan whole: no rule reads the file, so a finding of any rule in it is never
# reported, not only a finding of the audit its reason names. So a finding that belongs to
# one line is not entered here. It is marked in the file, with a nosemgrep comment on the
# line above the finding that names the one rule, under the reason the rule does not
# apply there, and every other rule still reads the file.
#
# The two paths left are the files the welcome image is made of. Each has one finding, and
# a comment added to either would change the image: welcome/VERSION records the hash of
# these files, a published version is never made of other files, and a changed hash takes
# the next version, which the merge then publishes. The reason above each path says which
# audit found what, and why that audit does not apply.
#
# The welcome image's program answers every request with its one page, a file embedded at
# build time that holds nothing a request sent, so the direct-write audit (an HTML page
# writing user data), which finds the write at the end of the handler, does not apply.
welcome/main.go
# The page's one link is its icon, written into the link itself as a data address:
# nothing is fetched, so there is nothing for an integrity attribute to check, and the
# missing-integrity audit, which finds that link, does not apply.
welcome/page.html
